    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.3.05-06",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Inbound international spending can contribute foreign exchange earnings, helping a country pay for imports or other external obligations. Domestic tourism primarily redistributes resident spending within the national economy. Both can support livelihoods. A stronger foreign-exchange claim needs information about receipts from abroad and payments abroad, rather than simply the presence of foreign visitors. Imported supplies or overseas payments can reduce the net foreign-exchange benefit.",
      "claim_ids": [
